& =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.5x+0.5xy)=0.5x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-5xy)=1-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5y & 0.5x \\\\ -5y & 1-5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5+0.5(1) & 0.5(0.2) \\\\ -5(1) & 1-5(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-5)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a38583d0-39a0-55f8-a293-360a29886e88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186514444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514532 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514532 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc9846dd-5c6b-5fee-8018-43e9e9a516aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514532 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186370186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514533 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186514532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514533 #edb/ref 1062 